/*Q12.
Sophia, a software developer, is working on a calendar application where accurate identification of leap years is crucial. She needs a program that efficiently checks whether a given year is a leap year. In the Gregorian calendar, a leap year is either divisible by 4 but not divisible by 100, or it is divisible by 400. Write a Java program that takes a year as input and determines whether the provided year is a leap year or not.

Input Format:
  - Single line contains an integer that represents the year

Output Format:
  - Single line displays whether the year is a leap year or not in the format: "<year> is Leap year" or "<year> is Not a leap year"

Sample Test Case 1:

    2024
    2024 is Leap year

Sample Test Case 2:

    1953
    1953 is Not a leap year

Note: A partial code is provided in the editor.*/
/////////////////////////////////////////////////////////////////////
/*Logic: Gregorian rule: divisible by 4 but not by 100, or divisible by 400. Note the exact text "Leap year" and "Not a leap year".*/

import java.util.Scanner;

public class Main {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        Scanner sc = new Scanner(System.in);
        int year = sc.nextInt();

        if ((year % 4 == 0 && year % 100 != 0) || year % 400 == 0) {
            System.out.println(year + " is Leap year");
        } else {
            System.out.println(year + " is Not a leap year");
        }
        sc.close();
    }
}
